.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt ED 1
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm ed
.Nd text editor
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl
.Op Fl s
.Op Fl p Ar string
.Op Ar file
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a line-oriented text editor.
It keeps a copy of a file in a buffer, changes it by commands read from the
standard input, and writes it back with
.Ic w .
With
.Ar file ,
it starts as though by
.Ic e Ar file .
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl p Ar string
Prompt with
.Ar string
before each command.
.It Fl s , Fl
Write no byte counts and no
.Ql \&!
after a shell command: for scripts.
.El
.Pp
A command is zero, one or two addresses, a letter, and arguments.
Most commands may be followed by
.Ic p ,
.Ic l
or
.Ic n ,
printing the current line afterwards in that form.
Commands
.Ic a ,
.Ic c
and
.Ic i
enter
.Em input mode :
lines are added to the buffer until one holding only a
.Ql \&. .
.Ss Addresses
The current line, usually the last one a command touched, is the default for
most commands.
An address is:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Li \&.
the current line
.It Li $
the last line
.It Ar n
line
.Ar n
.It Li ' Ns Ar x
the line marked
.Ar x
by
.Ic k
.It Li / Ns Ar re Ns Li /
the next line
.Ar re
matches, searching forward and wrapping around
.It Li \&? Ns Ar re Ns Li \&?
the previous one, searching backward
.It Li + Ns Ar n , Li - Ns Ar n , Li ^ Ns Ar n
an offset from the address before it, or from the current line; without
.Ar n ,
1
.El
.Pp
Two addresses separated by
.Ql \&,
are a range;
.Ql \&;
also makes the first one current before the second is evaluated.
.Ql \&,
or
.Ql %
alone is
.Li 1,$ ,
and
.Ql \&;
alone
.Li .,$ .
The empty regular expression
.Li //
is the last one used.
Regular expressions are basic; see
.Xr re_format 7 .
.Ss Commands
Default addresses are shown in parentheses.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pq Li \&. Ns Ic a
Append text after the line (0 for the start).
.It Pq Li \&.,.\& Ns Ic c
Change the lines to the text entered.
.It Pq Li \&.,.\& Ns Ic d
Delete the lines; the line after them becomes current.
.It Ic e Op Ar file
Edit
.Ar file ,
or the current file; warns once if the buffer has unsaved changes.
.It Ic E Op Ar file
As
.Ic e ,
without the warning.
.It Ic f Op Ar file
Set the current file name, and print it.
.It Pq Li 1,$ Ns Ic g Ns Li / Ns Ar re Ns Li / Ns Ar commands
Mark every line
.Ar re
matches, then run
.Ar commands
with each marked line current in turn.
Each line of
.Ar commands
but the last ends with a backslash; none means
.Ic p .
.It Pq Li 1,$ Ns Ic G Ns Li / Ns Ar re Ns Li /
Interactively: print each marked line and read a command to run on it; an empty
line does nothing,
.Ql &
repeats the last command.
.It Ic h
Explain the last error.
.It Ic H
Toggle explaining errors as they happen.
.It Pq Li \&. Ns Ic i
Insert text before the line.
.It Pq Li \&.,.+1\& Ns Ic j
Join the lines into one.
.It Pq Li \&. Ns Ic k Ns Ar x
Mark the line with the lower-case letter
.Ar x .
.It Pq Li \&.,.\& Ns Ic l
Print the lines unambiguously, as
.Xr sed 1 Ns 's
.Ic l .
.It Pq Li \&.,.\& Ns Ic m Ns Ar addr
Move the lines after
.Ar addr
(0 for the start).
.It Pq Li \&.,.\& Ns Ic n
Print the lines with their numbers.
.It Pq Li \&.,.\& Ns Ic p
Print the lines.
.It Ic P
Toggle the prompt
.Pq Ql * No unless set by Fl p .
.It Ic q
Quit; warns once if the buffer has unsaved changes.
.It Ic Q
Quit without the warning.
.It Pq Li $ Ns Ic r Op Ar file
Read
.Ar file
in after the line.
.It Pq Li \&.,.\& Ns Ic s Ns Li / Ns Ar re Ns Li / Ns Ar replacement Ns Li / Ns Ar flags
Substitute, as in
.Xr sed 1 :
.Ql &
and
.Li \e1
to
.Li \e9
in
.Ar replacement ,
a backslash before a newline splitting the line, and
.Ar replacement
.Ql %
alone the last one used.
The flags are
.Cm g ,
a count
.Ar N ,
and the print suffixes.
.Ic s
followed only by flags repeats the last substitution
.Cm ( g
toggling global replacement).
It is an error if no line changes.
.It Pq Li \&.,.\& Ns Ic t Ns Ar addr
Copy the lines after
.Ar addr .
.It Ic u
Undo the last command that changed the buffer (a
.Ic g
as a whole); another
.Ic u
redoes it.
.It Pq Li 1,$ Ns Ic v Ns Li / Ns Ar re Ns Li / Ns Ar commands
As
.Ic g ,
on the lines
.Ar re
does not match.
.It Pq Li 1,$ Ns Ic V Ns Li / Ns Ar re Ns Li /
As
.Ic G ,
likewise.
.It Pq Li 1,$ Ns Ic w Op Ar file
Write the lines to
.Ar file
or the current file.
.Ic wq
then quits.
.It Pq Li 1,$ Ns Ic W Op Ar file
Append the lines to the file.
.It Pq Li .+1 Ns Ic z Ns Op Ar n
Print
.Ar n
lines (22 by default) from the line.
.It Pq Li $ Ns Ic =
Print the line's number.
.It Ic \&! Ns Ar command
Run
.Ar command
with
.Xr sh 1 ;
.Ql %
in it is the current file name and a leading
.Ql \&!
the last command.
.It Pq Li .+1
An address alone prints that line.
.El
.Pp
.Ic e ,
.Ic r
and
.Ic w
take
.Ic \&! Ns Ar command
in place of a file, reading its output or writing to its input.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 on success.
When the standard input is not a terminal, an error ends
.Nm
at once with status 2; so does a
.Ar file
operand that can't be read.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Change every
.Ql foo
in a file to
.Ql bar
from a script:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf '%s\en' ',s/foo/bar/g' w q | ed -s file
.Ed
.Pp
Delete the comment lines:
.Dl g/^#/d
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
On an error
.Nm
writes
.Ql \&?
to the standard error, followed by an explanation after
.Ic H
(and, off a terminal, the script's line number).
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr grep 1 ,
.Xr sed 1 ,
.Xr sh 1 ,
.Xr re_format 7
.Sh STANDARDS
.Nm
conforms to IEEE Std 1003.1-2024 (\(lqPOSIX.1\(rq).
.Ic wq ,
.Ic z
and the
.Fl
option are those of the BSDs.
.Sh HISTORY
An
.Nm
command appeared in
.At v1 .
This one is written in Rust for OxideBSD.
